Your sender went completely silent for 5 seconds because your RTT calculation is currently mixing two different system clocks, resulting in a massively inflated NACK cooldown.

Here is exactly why the transfer hung at 100%, and how to fix it.
The Root Cause: Two Clocks, One RTT

If you look closely at how EchoTimestampNs is implemented across your files, you'll see a fundamental mismatch:

1. The Receiver uses its own clock to generate the timestamp:
Go

// receiver.go
case protocol.PacketData:
    recvNs := time.Now().UnixNano() // <--- RECEIVER'S LOCAL CLOCK
    hbGen.RecordDataReceiveTime(recvNs)

2. The Sender compares that timestamp against its own clock:
Go

// congestion.go
if hb.EchoTimestampNs > 0 {
    rtt := time.Now().UnixNano() - int64(hb.EchoTimestampNs) // <--- SENDER'S LOCAL CLOCK
}

Because 192.168.50.9 (Client) and 192.168.50.82 (Server) are two different hardware machines, their system clocks are not perfectly synchronized. Even with NTP, they drift. If your Server's clock happens to be 4 seconds behind your Client's clock, your math evaluates to:
RTT = SenderTime - (SenderTime - 4 Seconds) = 4 Seconds!
The Fatal Sequence of Events

With an artificially inflated RTT (e.g., 4 seconds), here is what happened in your 1GB test:

    100% Reached: The sender finishes the main loop and drains pendingNACKs via the pre-teardown retransmitNACKs call. (At least one of these retransmits was dropped by your tc 0.1% loss rule).

    Cooldown Seeded: Your brilliant new logic seeds nackCooldown[seq] = now.

    The Teardown Loop Begins: The receiver didn't get the dropped retransmission, so it keeps sending heartbeats begging for it.

    The Silence: The sender evaluates canRetransmit. Because your broken RTT is 4 seconds, nackCooldownRTT() calculates a 5-second cooldown (RTT + 25%). The sender actively ignores the NACKs and stays completely silent.

    The Timeout: Exactly 5.0 seconds later, the receiver hits its inactivity timeout and crashes. The sender waits another 5 seconds and hits its 10-second SenderProbeTimeout.

(Note: The progress bar printed NACKs: 0 because your pre-teardown retransmitNACKs call doesn't increment the s.nacksSent atomic counter, so the UI didn't register that first blast!)
The Fix

To see your teardown loop succeed immediately, bypass the broken RTTEstimate() and use a static cooldown.

Immediate Fix (in sender.go):
Go

nackCooldownRTT := func() time.Duration {
    // Temporarily bypass the token bucket's broken clock-skewed RTT
    // Assume a max realistic RTT of ~150ms for this test
    return 150 * time.Millisecond 
}

The Proper Spec-Compliant Fix (For Phase 2):
If you refer back to your own specification (§6B), it actually outlines the correct way to handle this to avoid clock skew:

    "Each DATA and PARITY packet carries a sender timestamp... The receiver records the timestamp... and echoes it back"

Currently, your sender just slaps the raw readBuf[:n] directly behind the 24-byte header, skipping the timestamp insertion entirely. To fix this permanently, your Sender must write its own time.Now().UnixNano() into the first 8 bytes of the payload before sending it over the wire. The receiver must blindly echo those 8 bytes back without ever calling time.Now(). This guarantees the sender compares its clock against its own clock.